  "panels": [
    {
      "title": "What this board is, and what it is not",
      "type": "text",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 10,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Two halves, and this board holds both.** *Who can do what* is the row directly below: users, roles, superusers and accounts nobody has ever signed into, read from the RBAC API, which is the half an access review asks for. *Who did what* is everything under it, read from PE's activity log.\n\n**Both halves are off by default and switched on here.** `pdctng::enable_rbac_metrics` and `pdctng::enable_console_activity_metrics` gate them, because these are the collectors that name people and who may read that is a site decision rather than ours to assume.\n\n**The counts start when pdctng starts, not when PE did.** PE keeps its activity log for a long time \u2014 thirteen months on the demo lab, with no sign of pruning \u2014 but the event counters here are tallied forward from the daemon's own start rather than replayed out of that history, so restarting the daemon resets them and Prometheus reads it as a counter reset. It also means these panels are quiet on a fresh install and fill in as the estate is used, which is the honest behaviour rather than an inherited backlog nobody did this week.\n\n**The cost is flat and stays flat.** The activity API has no time filter, but each response carries a running total, and that total is a cursor: the difference between one poll and the last is exactly how many events are new, so only those are read. An idle estate costs one request per service and a busy one costs two. Nothing on this board fans out per node.\n\n**What is still missing is the individual event.** These are counts, which Prometheus is good at; a single change with its subject, timestamp and source address is a log line, and one series per event would mint cardinality per event. That belongs in a log store and is the last row's subject.\n"
      },
      "id": 497422005
    },
    {
      "title": "1. Who can do what, right now",
      "type": "row",
      "collapsed": false,
      "gridPos": {
